    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 263 tests pass, including the new `AcceptanceTest.coreReturnsFiscalYear`. The `ERROR ExceptionControllerAdvice` line seen earlier is a pre-existing negative-path test that deliberately triggers an error \u2014 the build is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI moved all date-derived values onto a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starts 1 July, named by the calendar year it ends in \u2014 e.g. 2026-08-09 \u2192 `FY27`).\n\n**New shared utility** \u2014 `mapper/FiscalYear.java`:\n- `yearValue(date)` \u2192 fiscal year (month \u2265 July \u2192 `year + 1`, else `year`)\n- `label(date)` \u2192 `'FY<YY>'` (null-safe, so the field is omitted when there's no registration date)\n- `elapsed(from, to)` \u2192 count of fiscal years elapsed\n\n**Three behaviours now use it**, all keyed off the business-day-adjusted registration date the controller already stores on the owner:\n\n1. **New `fiscalYear` response field** \u2014 added to the `OwnerDto` schema in `openapi.yml` (generated into the DTO) and populated in `OwnerMapper` via `FiscalYear.label(owner.getRegistrationDate())`.\n2. **Membership identifier** \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.membershipNumber` now takes the `M<YY>` segment from the fiscal year rather than the raw calendar year (`'\u2026-M27'` for an Aug-2026 registration).\n3. **Tenure** \u2014 `MembershipPoints` awards the +3 points when `FiscalYear.elapsed(registrationDate, now) > 1` (more than one elapsed fiscal year) instead of the old 365-day window.\n\nI also updated the `membershipNumber` and `membershipPoints` descriptions in `openapi.yml` to document the fiscal-year basis.\n\n**One design decision worth flagging:** the spec says `'FY<YY>'` without pinning the convention, and the only test asserts the format `FY\\d{2}`. I chose the Australian-standard *ending-year* convention (FY2026\u201327 = `FY27`, matching the AU public-holiday data already in the codebase) and applied it consistently across all three outputs. For the tenure threshold I read \"exceeds 365 days\" \u2192 \"exceeds one fiscal year\" (`> 1` elapsed), which preserves the roughly-one-year-minimum intent. If you'd prefer the starting-year convention (`FY26`) or a `>= 1` tenure threshold, those are one-line changes in `FiscalYear`/`MembershipPoints`.",
